1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,492
Mahadev's ragi field dried out last week.

2
00:00:03,792 --> 00:00:07,332
He was away for three days, and
nobody switched on the pump.

3
00:00:07,682 --> 00:00:10,415
Could a small circuit do the job for him?

4
00:00:10,415 --> 00:00:12,984
That is exactly what electronics is for.

5
00:00:13,584 --> 00:00:14,983
Meet Project Neeru.

6
00:00:14,983 --> 00:00:19,616
Tanvi, a first-year engineering student
in Mandya, wants to build a smart

7
00:00:19,616 --> 00:00:22,110
water controller for her uncle's farm.

8
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:24,326
Imran builds the circuits.

9
00:00:24,326 --> 00:00:26,244
Lakshmi measures and codes.

10
00:00:26,594 --> 00:00:30,450
And Doctor Shabana, their lab
teacher, asks the hard questions.

11
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:32,739
So what is electronics?

12
00:00:33,039 --> 00:00:34,922
Electricity is moving charge.

13
00:00:34,922 --> 00:00:37,386
It can light a bulb or run a motor.

14
00:00:37,736 --> 00:00:42,410
Electronics uses that current to
carry information and make decisions.

15
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:45,985
In simple words: electrical
power does the work.

16
00:00:45,985 --> 00:00:48,182
Electronics decides when and how.

17
00:00:48,832 --> 00:00:51,321
The information travels as a signal.

18
00:00:51,321 --> 00:00:55,101
A signal is a quantity that
changes to carry a message.

19
00:00:55,451 --> 00:00:59,976
An analog signal changes smoothly,
like the soil getting drier hour by

20
00:00:59,976 --> 00:01:00,876
hour.

21
00:01:00,906 --> 00:01:04,122
A digital signal has only
two levels, on and off.

22
00:01:04,122 --> 00:01:06,175
We write them as one and zero.

23
00:01:06,825 --> 00:01:08,780
Signals flow through components.

24
00:01:09,130 --> 00:01:13,535
Passive components, like resistors
and capacitors, cannot add energy.

25
00:01:13,535 --> 00:01:15,777
They resist, store or guide it.

26
00:01:16,127 --> 00:01:20,186
Active components, like transistors
and chips, can control the current

27
00:01:20,186 --> 00:01:21,866
and make a signal bigger.

28
00:01:22,516 --> 00:01:26,068
Put components together for a
purpose, and you get a system.

29
00:01:26,068 --> 00:01:27,928
Every system does three things.

30
00:01:28,278 --> 00:01:29,178
Sense.

31
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:31,950
A sensor turns the real
world into a signal.

32
00:01:32,250 --> 00:01:33,150
Decide.

33
00:01:32,891 --> 00:01:35,638
A circuit compares the signal with a rule.

34
00:01:35,938 --> 00:01:36,838
Act.

35
00:01:36,393 --> 00:01:39,567
An output, like a pump or
a light, does the work.

36
00:01:40,217 --> 00:01:42,286
Let's watch Project Neeru do it.

37
00:01:42,636 --> 00:01:44,590
At sunrise the soil is wet.

38
00:01:44,590 --> 00:01:46,175
The sensor reads high.

39
00:01:46,175 --> 00:01:47,611
The pump stays off.

40
00:01:47,961 --> 00:01:51,490
By noon the soil dries, and
the signal falls, slowly.

41
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:53,576
It crosses the dry line.

42
00:01:53,576 --> 00:01:55,760
The circuit decides: water now.

43
00:01:56,110 --> 00:01:59,689
The relay clicks, the pump runs,
and the green light comes on.

44
00:02:00,039 --> 00:02:04,493
Water soaks in, the signal climbs
back, and the pump switches off on its

45
00:02:04,493 --> 00:02:05,393
own.

46
00:02:04,977 --> 00:02:06,592
Nobody had to be there.

47
00:02:07,242 --> 00:02:09,265
Electrical power does the work.

48
00:02:09,265 --> 00:02:11,181
Electronics does the thinking.

49
00:02:11,881 --> 00:02:13,849
Where do we see this at work today?

50
00:02:14,199 --> 00:02:15,099
Sense.

51
00:02:14,814 --> 00:02:19,523
A hospital monitor reads a patient's
heartbeat through sensors on the skin.

52
00:02:19,873 --> 00:02:20,773
Decide.

53
00:02:20,587 --> 00:02:25,664
A car's airbag circuit decides in a
split second whether a crash is real.

54
00:02:26,014 --> 00:02:26,914
Act.

55
00:02:26,537 --> 00:02:30,974
A phone screen turns a signal into
millions of tiny dots of light.

56
00:02:31,624 --> 00:02:33,925
But is more electronics always better?

57
00:02:34,275 --> 00:02:35,403
The good side.

58
00:02:35,403 --> 00:02:38,118
It saves water, time, and even lives.

59
00:02:38,468 --> 00:02:39,625
The hard side.

60
00:02:39,625 --> 00:02:43,781
It needs power, it can fail,
and old gadgets become e-waste.

61
00:02:44,131 --> 00:02:48,746
In twenty twenty two, the world threw
away about sixty two million tonnes

62
00:02:48,746 --> 00:02:50,187
of electronic waste.

63
00:02:50,187 --> 00:02:52,979
Less than a quarter was properly recycled.

64
00:02:53,329 --> 00:02:57,300
Mahadev asks: what if the sensor
breaks, and the pump never stops?

65
00:02:57,650 --> 00:02:59,612
So Tanvi adds a safety timer.

66
00:02:59,612 --> 00:03:03,261
The pump can never run for
more than one hour at a time.

67
00:03:03,611 --> 00:03:04,562
The lesson?

68
00:03:04,562 --> 00:03:08,116
Good electronics also plans
for when things go wrong.

69
00:03:08,766 --> 00:03:09,702
Quick check.

70
00:03:09,702 --> 00:03:12,239
A soil sensor, a circuit and a pump.

71
00:03:12,239 --> 00:03:13,522
Which one decides?

72
00:03:15,422 --> 00:03:16,322
The circuit!

73
00:03:16,311 --> 00:03:19,932
The sensor senses, the circuit
decides, and the pump acts.

74
00:03:20,532 --> 00:03:21,544
So, remember.

75
00:03:21,844 --> 00:03:26,075
Electronics uses current to carry
information and make decisions.

76
00:03:26,425 --> 00:03:28,558
Signals are analog or digital.

77
00:03:28,558 --> 00:03:30,848
Components are passive or active.

78
00:03:31,198 --> 00:03:34,035
And every system senses, decides and acts.

79
00:03:34,635 --> 00:03:37,812
Next time: voltage,
current, power and energy.
